g segments. The company is involved in the discovery and development of recording artists, as well as related marketing, promotion, distribution, sale, and licensing of music created by recording artists; markets its music catalog through compilations and reissuances of previously released music and video titles, as well as previously unreleased materials. It also owns and acquires rights to approximately two million musical compositions comprising pop hits, American standards, folk songs, and motion picture and theatrical compositions, as well as administers the music and soundtracks of various third-party television and film producers and studios. In addition, the company conducts its operation primarily through a collection of record labels, such as Asylum, Big Beat, Canvasback, East West, Erato, FFRR, Nonesuch, Parlophone, Reprise, Sire, Spinnin' Records, and Warner Classics and Warner Records Nashville. Further, it markets, distributes, and sells music and video products to retailers and wholesale distributors; independent labels to retail and wholesale distributors; and various distribution centers and ventures, as well as retail outlets, online physical retailers, streaming services, and download services. Its catalog includes songwriters and composers; and various genres, including pop, rock, jazz, classical, country, R&B, hip-hop, rap, reggae, Latin, folk, alternative, blues, gospel, and other Christian music. The company was founded in 1929 and is headquartered in New York, New York.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Robert  Kyncl | President, CEO & Director | Age 54 | Compensation: $5.2M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 5K people. Revenue per employee of $1.2M ranks in the top quartile among Communication Services peers, with revenue growing 7%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $98K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $10K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (7/10).","financial_summary":["Warner Music Group Corp. is a large-cap communication services business with a $16.9B market cap on $6.7B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +4% over the past three years and +9% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 16% against a 9% cost of capital, a 8-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it.","The business throws off $539M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 8 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.86\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 3.3\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage runs at 3.9\u00d7.","Shareholder yield of 2.4% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 64% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.6356299369665009},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.7) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 6.4% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Advertising-cycle exposure and content/regulatory risk \u2014 a single platform policy shift or ad-spend contraction cascades through the P&L quickly.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Cord-cutting acceleration and audience fragmentation \u2014 legacy distribution bundles unravel faster than subscription revenue can replace them.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"AI-generated content commoditisation \u2014 as creation costs collapse toward zero, content libraries lose pricing power and differentiation narrows.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Barrow Hanley Mewhinney & Strauss LLC Reports 4.29% Ownership Stake in Warner Music Group","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/news/announcements/barrow-hanley-mewhinney-strauss-llc-reports-429-ownership-stake-in-warner-music-group","date":"2026-11-12","timestamp":1794499279.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":1,"scope":"company","tags":["m_and_a"]},{"title":"Chase Carey Sells $62M Of Liberty Media As Shares Trade 1.17% Above Sale Price","source":"benzinga.com","link":"https://www.benzinga.com/trading-ideas/insider-trades/26/11/63248683/chase-carey-sells-62m-of-liberty-media-as-shares-trade-117-above-sale-price","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794585828.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.681,"tier":2,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Why Elvis Presley\u2019s and Eminem\u2019s music owners are suing Suno","source":"thenextweb.com","link":"https://thenextweb.com/news/suing-suno-elvis-presley-eminem-lawsuits-elvis-act","date":"2026-11-12","timestamp":1794482256.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":-0.681,"tier":2,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Music Stocks To Watch Now \u2013 November 9th","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"